Lunar Funeral Hymn (Domain Expansion)
Executive Summary
Lunar Funeral Hymn is a new, canon-compatible Domain Expansion for a powerful sorceress, tuned to have a ~65:35 advantage over Sukuna’s Malevolent Shrine. Like Sukuna’s domain, it creates an open area (no barrier)[1], flooding the surroundings with necromantic, moonlit water. The caster commands one of 8 distinct moon phases (Blood, Blue, Wolf, Snow, Harvest, Hunter’s, Cold, Crow), each granting unique supernatural effects. Sound is the central mechanic: cursed speech, whistles, chanting, and bell-tones both direct spectral allies and unleash tangible sonic attacks (slicing blades, shockwaves, etc.), leveraging JJK’s concept of voice-as-curse[2].
Spirits and shinigami arise from the water to tug at souls and weaken foes, much like Abhorsen bells bind the dead[3]. The domain also grants the caster a black thistle polearm (inspired by Garth Nix) which serves as a melee weapon and sound amplifier. Lunar Funeral Hymn is carefully balanced: its vast area and guaranteed-hit effects[4] rival Sukuna’s, but we include counters and costs to prevent trivial wins. Nevertheless, its design advantage (65%) comes from added phase versatility, spirit control, and the lethal thistle weapon.
This report details: the activation ritual, domain rules, sound-attack mechanics (with an effects mapping diagram), phase abilities (with scaling tiers and examples), spirit types and soul-tugging, interaction with Sukuna’s domain (showing how Lunar Funeral Hymn wins ~65% of clashes), limitations and counters, and bell/chant motifs. We also describe the black thistle polearm (material, magic, use) and provide tables and mermaid diagrams for clarity. All mechanics are grounded in Jujutsu Kaisen canon[4][1] and Abhorsen lore[3][5].
Activation Ritual & Core Rules
•	Invocation: I light a single black candle at midnight and strike a deep tolling bell. Under this somber peal, I recite a short incantation (e.g. “Lunae mors, venit umbra” – “Moon of death, let the shadow come”) while slowly whistling a mournful tune. This can be done normally or in duress: as a backup, a piercing whistle or shouted chant suffices to trigger the domain even under attack.
•	Non-Enclosed Space: Upon activation, the domain spreads immediately through the real environment[1]. Ethereal water rises (coating floors and objects) and the sky darkens to reveal the chosen moon phase. No barrier walls appear; instead the entire area is haunted by the domain’s water and sky. This mimics Sukuna’s innate domain which is “painted on air”[1], allowing enemies to move freely while still affected.
•	Phases of the Moon: I choose one of 8 moon phases (listed below) during the incantation. The sky shifts accordingly (e.g. a fiery red for Blood Moon) and sustains that phase. Changing the phase mid-battle requires a quick additional chant and extra energy, risking concentration. Each phase modifies the domain’s effects significantly (detailed later).
•	Guaranteed Hits: All offensive actions tied to the domain (sonic blasts, spirit strikes, etc.) automatically hit targets within range, reflecting the guaranteed-hit rule of lethal domains[4]. For example, if I send forth a Sound Slash or throw the thistle spear with a battle cry, it cannot miss. This is a fundamental aspect, akin to Sukuna’s assured slashes[4].
•	Area and Scale (Tiered): The domain’s effective radius is tiered by energy. Tier 1 (~20–50m) floods a single building; Tier 2 (~100–150m) spans multiple blocks; Tier 3 (~200m+) can cover a large urban area (on par with Sukuna’s domain[1]). Enemies farther out feel the pull of the cursed moonlight (slowed movement, chilling sensation) but are safe from direct effects.
•	Water of Death: The domain’s water continuously saps cursed energy and vitality from foes wading in it. It acts like an aura: each round spent immersed weakens enemies by a small percent of their energy. This compounding drain fits the necromantic theme and helps ensure the final “soul-tug” kill.
Black Thistle Polearm
•	Design & Lore: The caster wields a dark thistle polearm (inspired by Garth Nix’s free-magic flora). It’s a long, blackwood shaft wrapped in thorny iron, with a dual-ended spearhead. Like Abhorsen’s bells forged in Death’s waters[5], this polearm is forged in necromantic rites – its wood was quenched in the “Waters of Death,” binding it to the domain’s magic. It’s said that the thistle metal is infused with free-magic poison, making it lethal to spirits and mortal alike.
•	Melee Use: In melee, the polearm deals heavy slash/pierce damage. It can impale cursed spirits or fend off enemies trying to close in. Every strike is imbued with the domain’s power: even a glancing blow summons tiny spectral thorns that latch onto the victim, continuing to drain health (similar to Sukuna’s slashes causing aftereffects[6]).
•	Sound Conduction: The polearm doubles as an amplifier. It has hollows or runes along the shaft that resonate with the caster’s voice. By chanting into the pole, I can launch focused sound lances: e.g. speaking “Spatium!” through it sends a tapered sonic spear that tears through the air. The weapon essentially works like a blowgun or resonator.
•	Ranged Capability: The caster can also throw the polearm. When thrown with a chant, it returns (like a boomerang), trailing a spiral of cursed energy. It creates a devastating Shockwave Explosion on impact (as if the pole violently resonates upon hitting ground). This gives a powerful ranged option, yet the pole always returns to her hand magically.
•	Special Attack – Thistle Bloom: She can strike the ground with the pole, causing ghostly thistle vines to erupt in an area. These vines entangle and spike foes (a crowd-control zone). This is a signature move requiring a loud shout (“Spina regnant! – “Thorns reign!”) and a vigorous slam.
Sound-Based Attack Mechanics
All attacks in Lunar Funeral Hymn use sound as their medium. Cursed speech (voice), whistles, and bells function like spells, each with JJK analogues:
•	Cursed Commands: Short shouts or words carry cursed force. For instance:
•	“Stay!” freezes a target’s feet in swirling water (akin to a binding domain rule).
•	“Shatter!” emits a concussive blast that can destroy obstacles (similar in effect to Sukuna’s Divine Flame that pulverizes surroundings[7], but here acoustically).
•	Whistles & Chants: Melodic tones produce elemental effects:
•	A high-pitched whistle creates a Frostblade (freezing sonic arc) under Snow Moon.
•	A low chant booms out a Soundquake (rock-throwing quake wave).
These are analogous to known techniques (e.g. Jogo’s powerful sound). Each is scalable by tier (Tier 1: small jolt; Tier 3: city-shaking boom).
•	Bell-Tones: Three ceremonial bells convey classic Abhorsen functions[8][9]:
•	Binder Bell (deep tone) forces obedience or paralysis (like “No Escape” boundary).
•	Sleeper Bell (gentle tone) casts sleep.
•	Grave Bell (bassy funeral toll) drains life from enemies in range.
Striking combinations yields compound effects.
•	Attack Types:
•	Sound Slash: Swinging the polearm with a yell (“Cut!”) sends a blade of compressed sound. This acts like a ranged slash attack with enhanced cut-through (cannot miss, guaranteed to draw blood).
•	Echo Sphere: Whistling forms a shimmering sphere of sonic energy around a point. After a brief flash, it implodes like a grenade, damaging all within (area-stun).
•	Roaring Wave: A roar amplifies through the domain’s water to create a wall of shock-energy that sweeps forward, toppling anyone hit (similar to a large-scale wind blast).
