Spirits & Soul-Tugging
•	Wraiths and Revenants: The domain’s water teems with dormant spirit-forms. With certain sounds, I can call them forth: for example, a harsh whistle might animate a water-wraith that grips a foe from below. These spirits latch onto enemies’ souls. Each successful latch drains an extra chunk of vitality (like an ongoing life-drain).
•	Gossamer Shade: A fleeting, nearly invisible spirit that flits behind a target, dimming their senses. It halves the target’s reaction speed each round until dispelled.
•	Thorn Wraith: A bulky specter of tangled thorns (ties in with the polearm theme) that ensnares anyone in melee range, enabling a guaranteed follow-up strike by the caster.
•	Soul Extraction: Once a foe is sufficiently weakened (near 0 HP or cursed energy drained), I can order a spirit: e.g. “Absorbe!”. A spirit will attempt to tug the soul out. This is a domain-executive move – if successful, the enemy is slain outright. Against Sukuna-tier foes, it might require 2-3 consecutive tugs (Sukuna’s heavy cursed energy gives partial resistance). The tug is faster and stronger under Cold and Crow Moons (as fits their themes). Failing a tug attempt forces me to wait one turn before trying again, preventing instant kills.
Interaction with Malevolent Shrine
•	Domain Clash: In a clash, Lunar Funeral Hymn is designed to win ~65% of the time. This is achieved by both mechanical edge and narrative factors:
•	Wide Area Advantage: At equal tier, Lunar Hymn can extend slightly beyond Sukuna’s shrine radius. E.g. if both are Tier 3, Lunar Hymn might cover 220m vs Sukuna’s 200m[1], letting it strike Sukuna’s domain from outside.
•	Sound vs Slash: Since Sukuna’s shrine relies on slashes, Lunar’s water can partially dampen those slashes. We assume that in 65% of equal-energy clashes, Sukuna’s slashes are slightly deflected by the cursed water (forcing him to use more energy per slash). Conversely, Lunar’s sonic weapons go straight through Sukuna’s domain because sound isn’t blocked by the open space.
•	Domain Primacy: If both domains fully overlap, Lunar Hymn’s phase abilities give it the upper hand. For example, under Cold Moon, Sukuna’s movements slow, reducing the speed of his slashes. Under Crow Moon, summoned crows might even distract Sukuna momentarily (in roleplay). We assign a slight edge: whenever a random factor is needed, think Lunar with 65% win-chance.
•	Guaranteed Hits: Each domain still guarantees hits within its area[4]. If Lunar Hymn uses a spiritual attack on Sukuna himself, it still hits (just as his shrine attacks foes).
•	Binding Oath: Narratively, Lunar Hymn’s caster might have a specialized binding vow: e.g. “I vow this domain shall bind the blood-spirit of any who wield a shrine.” This domain-specific vow (unique to her cursed technique) means that if Sukuna tries a world-cutting slash outside Lunar’s area, the polearm or a spirit thwarts it (reflecting the 65% scenario advantage). This mystical “out-of-domain” defense is not explicit in canon, but it models the designed advantage.
Limitations, Counters & Fail-Safes
•	Voice Silence: Completely sealing the caster’s voice (gagging, neck attack) instantly collapses the domain (no sound → no domain).
•	Material Weakness: The black thistle polearm, while powerful, is itself vulnerable: a strike of white-hot inferno can cause it to crumble (thorns burn). Against enemies like Jogo, using the poleam might risk destroying it.
•	Energy Drain: Casting strong commands can cause backlash: after a high-power shout, the caster’s throat might be raw (akin to Inumaki’s cough-up-blood[10]). Intensive use of bells also exhausts her.
•	Cursed Speech Limits: Like canon, one should not speak casually—every word is magical. Accidental utterance of a bound command (e.g. saying “Sleep” during conversation) could backfire.
•	Domain Duration: The domain will last until the caster is incapacitated or chooses to end it. However, prolonged maintenance (beyond 10 minutes of real time) is extremely taxing and may cause collapse.
•	Counters: Characters like Gojo could use spatial techniques to minimize domain area. Enemies with purified sound nullifying tools (amulets, barriers) can reduce the domain’s reach. Nevertheless, even when sound is blocked, the water drain still works, so only a well-prepared sorcerer stands a chance.
•	Balance: To avoid trivializing, the soul-tug cannot affect Sukuna unless he’s below a threshold (e.g. ~10% HP), and phases do not guarantee insta-kills—they add effects but don’t outright cancel domain rules. In narrative, allow heroic counters (like teamwork, off-domain retreats, trickery) to even odds sometimes.
Sample Chants & Bell Motifs
•	Activation: “Umbra lunare, regna domus!” (“Lunar shadow, rule this home!”) — domain birth.
•	Binder Bell: Deep toll – “Obediat!” (“Obey!”) – forces compliance/paralysis.
•	Sleeper Bell: Soft chime – “Dormias!” (“Sleep!”) – induces sleep on the weary.
•	Thistle Strike: “Spinae!” (“Thorns!”) – strike the pole, sending thorn-wraith.
•	Blood Call: “Sanguinem!” (“Blood!”) – releases red waves on targets.
•	Hunter Cry: “Prey io!” (“I hunt!”) – places a cursed mark on enemy.
•	Wolf Howl: “Lupus!” (“Wolf!”) – summons wolf spirits under Wolf Moon.
•	Crow Toll: “Funera!” (“Death!”) – unleashes a murder of crows.
Each pattern is short (1–3 syllables) to focus power[10]. The bells follow Abhorsen motifs (binding, sleep, death).
