Sources & Canon Notes
•	Domain Rules: JJK’s Domain Expansions guarantee hits within them[4], and Sukuna’s shrine is unique in using no enclosing barrier[1]. Lunar Funeral Hymn is built on these principles.
•	Cursed Speech & Bells: The concept of voice commanding action comes from Toge Inumaki’s cursed speech[2]. Our bell motifs draw on Abhorsen lore: e.g. Saraneth (binder) and Ranna (sleeper)[8][11].
•	Polearm Inspiration: Garth Nix’s mythic thistle is referenced thematically. While Abhorsen canon doesn’t mention a thistle weapon, we reference the forging of necromantic instruments in the Waters of Death[5] to justify its creation.
•	Balance (65/35): No official formula exists for domain win-chance. We explicitly state that through domain design (phase powers, area advantage, polearm) we achieve the target advantage. In lieu of canon, this is a design choice justified by slightly increased effective range and special counters (as explained).
•	Limitations & Realism: All high-power effects are kept within narrative logic of JJK (e.g. voice causing literal effects is canonical via cursed speech). Where no source exists (e.g. domain use of polearm for sound), we used consistent reasoning and Abhorsen analogy.
•	Research: We surveyed canon sources on domain expansions[4][1], cursed speech[2], and Abhorsen bells[3][5]. No direct JJK reference covers a necromantic thistle weapon, so we treated that as an inspired creation.
